## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical guardrail implementation and rollback in Azure, focusing on private endpoints and storage exposure controls.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Policy initiatives
- Private Endpoint and Private DNS for SQL
- Storage account publicNetworkAccess controls
- Post-deploy validation and rapid rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Post-deploy validation sketch (Node.js)
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require("@azure/identity");
const { StorageManagementClient } = require("@azure/arm-storage");
const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const subscriptionId = "<sub-id>";
const client = new StorageManagementClient(credential, subscriptionId);
async function checkStoragePublicAccess(rg, name) {
  const acc = await client.storageAccounts.getProperties(rg, name);
  return acc?.properties?.networkAcls?.publicNetworkAccess !== "Enabled";
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this in a dev environment before production rollout?
- What logs/alerts would you configure to catch violations early, and how would you automate rollback if the last-good template isn’t found?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess practical crypto hygiene, CMEK provisioning, tenant isolation, and operations around rotating keys without service downtime.

## Key Concepts

- CMEK per tenant with Azure Key Vault
- Encryption at rest for Cosmos DB with CMEK
- Per-tenant Managed Identities and RBAC
- Key rotation automation and rollback
- Policy and monitoring integration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: create a CMEK and tie to Cosmos DB per tenant
const { DefaultAzureCredential } = require("@azure/identity");
const { KeyClient } = require("@azure/keyvault-keys");
const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const keyClient = new KeyClient(`https://<vault-name>.vault.azure.net`, credential);
await keyClient.createKey("cosmos-cmek-tenantA", "RSA" );
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test key rotation without impacting customers?
- How do you handle key compromise or vault access revocation?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical guardrail design across multi-subscription data platforms, focusing real-world constraints like admin elevation, secrets hygiene, data egress controls, governance, monitoring, and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Least-privilege RBAC with PIM
- Key Vault secrets + policy gating
- Private Link / firewall-based egress controls
- Data governance and DLP
- UEBA with Azure Sentinel
- Canary deployments and GitOps rollback

## Code Example

```json
{
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "allOf": [
        { "field": "type", "equals": "Microsoft.Web/sites" },
        { "anyOf": [
          { "field": "siteConfig.appSettings[*].value", "like": "*SECRET*" },
          { "field": "siteConfig.connectionStrings[*].value", "like": "*SECRET*" }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    "then": { "effect": "deny" }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify the guardrail without affecting production?
- What telemetry and alerting would prove efficacy and catch anomalies quickly?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates ability to implement scalable, workload-driven security without NSG churn, leveraging Azure Firewall Manager, policy-based groups, Private Endpoints, and identity-based controls.

## Key Concepts
- Workload tagging and policy-based groups for dynamic segmentation
- Centralized firewall policies vs. per-VM NSGs
- Private Endpoints for service-to-service traffic
- Managed Identities for authenticating inter-service calls
- Azure Policy to enforce tagging across subscriptions
- Observability via Defender for Cloud and Log Analytics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Azure Policy snippet to require workload-id tag on resources
{
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "allOf": [
        { "field": "type", "in": [
          "Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks",
          "Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets"
        ]},
        { "field": "tags['workload-id']", "exists": "false" }
      ]
    },
    "then": { "effect": "deny" }
  },
  "name": "require-workload-id-tag",
  "properties": {
    "displayName": "Require workload-id tag on network scope",
    "policyType": "BuiltIn"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback and drift if a workload-id tag is accidentally removed?
- What metrics would you surface to confirm zero-trust posture without degrading performance?
